          "title" : "Bringing radio frequency and infrared devices into facilities",
          "props" : [ {
            "name" : "sort-id",
            "value" : "catalog[1].group[07].group[1].group[4]"
          } ],
          "parts" : [ {
            "name" : "overview",
            "prose" : "Radio frequency (RF) devices, such as mobile devices, wireless keyboards and Bluetooth devices, as well as infrared (IR) devices, can pose a security risk to an organisation, especially when they can record or transmit audio or data. In SECRET and TOP SECRET areas, it is important that an organisation understands the security risks associated with the introduction of RF and IR devices and develop, implement, maintain and regularly verify a register of those that have been authorised for use in such environments.\n\nIn deciding which RF or IR devices to authorise to be brought into SECRET and TOP SECRET areas, an organisation should consider any mitigating measures already in place. These include whether IR communications would be prevented from travelling outside secured spaces, whether systems of different sensitivities or classifications are used in the same spaces, and if any temporary or permanent method of blocking RF or IR transmissions has been applied to the facility."
          } ],
