      "title" : "Guidelines for evaluated products",
      "props" : [ {
        "name" : "sort-id",
        "value" : "catalog[1].group[12]"
      } ],
      "groups" : [ {
        "title" : "Evaluated product procurement",
        "props" : [ {
          "name" : "sort-id",
          "value" : "catalog[1].group[12].group[1]"
        } ],
        "parts" : [ {
          "name" : "overview",
          "prose" : "# Context\n\nAn evaluated product provides a level of assurance in its security functionality that an unevaluated product does not. Evaluation documentation can assist an organisation in assessing the suitability of products and making informed procurement and deployment decisions.\n\nThe Australian Certification Authority within the Australian Signals Directorate (ASD) certifies product evaluations conducted by licensed commercial facilities, in accordance with the Common Criteria (i.e. the International Organization for Standardization/International Electrotechnical Commission 15408 series), as part of the Australian Information Security Evaluation Program (AISEP).\n\nIn support of Common Criteria evaluations, a Protection Profile (PP) is a technology-specific document that defines the security functionality that must be included in a Common Criteria evaluated product to mitigate specific cyber threats. PPs can support Secure by Design principles and practices by defining security functional and assurance requirements for classes of products against which individual products can be evaluated. PPs can be published by a recognised Common Criteria Recognition Arrangement (CCRA) scheme or by the CCRA body itself. PPs published by the CCRA body are referred to as collaborative PPs. ASD recognises all collaborative PPs listed on the [Common Criteria](#231b9f0c-6f88-4ead-ac34-acd2a24a0008) website, and will consider national PPs listed on the United States’ [National Information Assurance Partnership](#15d9668e-8aa4-4f80-b6e5-05400621cfb4) website, in addition to those listed on ASD’s [AISEP](#57c58870-2ebb-4fce-b995-0c86cb64002f) webpage. Where a PP does not exist, an evaluation based on an Evaluation Assurance Level (EAL) may be accepted. Such evaluations are capped at EAL2+ as this represents the best balance between completion time and meaningful security assurance gains.\n\nIn addition, some CCRA schemes leverage the [Cryptographic Algorithm Validation Program](#42e7d99d-9709-4355-a54d-98959c552aa1) for the validation of cryptographic algorithm implementations used by cryptographic modules within evaluated products. In such cases, cryptographic algorithm testing is performed by Cryptographic and Security Testing laboratories that are accredited by the United States’ National Voluntary Laboratory Accreditation Program.\n\nFinally, ASD also performs evaluations for products used to protect SECRET and TOP SECRET data via its High Assurance Evaluation Program.\n\n# Further information\n\nFurther information on the [High Assurance Evaluation Program](#e4ef23da-2683-4356-93c4-a2412c9d52ee) is available from ASD.\n\nFurther information on the [AISEP](#57c58870-2ebb-4fce-b995-0c86cb64002f) is available from ASD.\n\nFurther information on Common Criteria evaluated products can be found on the Common Criteria’s [Certified Products List](#29b04eaf-5f57-4ea1-a453-8047826dccf5).\n\nFurther information on cyber supply chain risk management can be found in the ‘Cyber supply chain risk management’ section of the [Guidelines for procurement and outsourcing](#f37a4848-0791-4870-b316-5536c2681c28)."
        } ],
        "groups" : [ {
          "title" : "Evaluated product selection",
          "props" : [ {
            "name" : "sort-id",
            "value" : "catalog[1].group[12].group[1].group[1]"
